<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dudo of Saint-Quentin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 965–before 1043</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dudo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> was a canon from the Vermandois who came to the Norman court as an envoy and, at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and Richard II, wrote a history of the first rulers of Normandy, De moribus et actis primorum Normanniae ducum. Completed around 1015, it gives a celebratory, partly legendary account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> and his successors and is the main source for early Norman history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dudo was a Picard, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> in the 960s, and later became dean there. He was a learned man who probably received his education at Liège or perhaps at Laon. By 987 he had become a canon of St Quentin, whose abbacy was held by the counts of Vermandois. Very little else is known about his life beyond his work for the Norman court and the fact that he died before 1043.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 987 Albert I, Count of Vermandois, sent Dudo on a diplomatic mission to Richard I of Normandy, and the mission succeeded. In the two years before Richard's death in 996 Dudo was a frequent visitor to the Norman court. In a letter to Adalbero, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Laon, he wrote that Richard had asked him to record the customs and deeds of the Norman land and the rights established in the kingdom of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>. He wrote the history during a second stay in Normandy, as the duke had urged him to do.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Historia Normannorum, also called the Gesta Normannorum, was written between 996 and 1015 and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalberon-bishop-of-laon">Adalbero of Laon</a>. It is divided into four parts, alternates between prose and verse in several metres, and covers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> from 852 to the death of Duke Richard in 996. Dudo knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s Aeneid and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> authors, but his Latin is affected and obscure. He seems to have used no earlier documents, relying on oral tradition, much of it supplied by Raoul, Count of Ivry, a maternal half-brother of Duke Richard.</p><p>Because of this the work reads in places like a romance. Ernst Dümmler and Georg Waitz judged it untrustworthy, while Jules Lair and Johannes Steenstrup, admitting that it mixes in legend, valued it as a source. Leah Shopkow has argued that Carolingian saints' lives, the Vita S. Germani of Heiric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a> and the Vita S. Lamberti of Stephen of Liège, served as models. Dudo also claims that Richard I was sent to learn the Dacian, or Old Norse, tongue, and that the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> spoke it more often than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-jumieges">William of Jumièges</a>, Wace, Robert of Torigni, William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and Hugh of Fleury drew heavily on the work for their own chronicles. André Duchesne first printed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1619; later editions appeared in Migne's Patrologia Latina in 1844 and, edited by Jules Lair, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> in 1865.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dudo of Saint-Quentin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
